  "Source": "R. J. Holloway and P. H. Lissberger, \"The Design and Preparation of Induced Transmission Filters,\" Appl. Opt. 8, 653-660 (1969). doi:10.1364/AO.8.000653 - Fig. 3(c) label and caption, p. 655",
  "Notes": "Double half-wave (two-cavity) all-dielectric comparison filter at 2 um (Fig. 3(c))\nSpecification: Two-cavity (double half-wave) all-dielectric filter used only for comparison.\nReported: Fig. 3(c) (calculated, plot only): psi = 1; tau0' and 1/(1+F sin^2 phi) are both resonance functions peaking at 2.0 um, giving a narrow pass band at 2.0 um with sidebands near about 1.6 and 2.6 um and a wide blocking region between about 1.6-1.9 and 2.1-2.5 um (approximate plot reading).\nNotes: Label read from a 1200-dpi render of the small figure legend: glass|LHLHL|HH|LHLHLHL'HL'|HH|L'HL'H|glass, with mu_H = 4.1, mu_L = 2.29, mu_L' = 2.426, mu_g = 1.5. Each HH is one 2-QWOT layer, giving 20 layers; listed as printed, assuming light from the left glass (normal incidence, both media glass). The primes are small and the reading of the middle group (L H L H L H L' H L') is the best interpretation of the print; a recomputation that does not show a clean pass band at 2.0 um should first suspect this transcription. Design wavelength 2.0 um assumed from the plot. Constant indices.\nLayers from Fig. 3(c) label and caption, p. 655; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nNot checked: the paper reports this design only as plots.",
